WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.160
There’s a 
chance that before you had an idea of what

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:24.380
an amoeba was, you had an image of them in
your head, built on headlines describing invisible

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:31.380
brain-eaters or pop culture references to
their simple, amorphous bodies.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:40.960
Tardigrades and hydras and stentors are magnificent,
but amoebas…they’re classic.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:48.120
When asked how he named his iconic California
record store Amoeba Music, co-owner Marc Weinstein

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:55.160
said, “The word "amoeba" came up because
it sounded good with ‘music.’

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.460
We just wanted it to be a psychedelic kind
of name that students would relate to.”

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.210
Record stores aren’t alone in finding familiarity
with amoebas.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:12.410
They show up often in writing, like a cultural
ambassador of the microcosmos that serves

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:19.430
as a handy reference for anything that is
blobby, that splits, that is undeveloped.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:27.040
But as is the truth of many a muse, the reality
of amoebas is so much more complicated and

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:34.690
weird than the flattened image of a formless
ancestor that we project our literary needs

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.020
onto.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:44.310
The very idea of “amoeba” as a category
is a relic from the 19th century classification

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:50.740
of single-celled eukaryotes, or protists,
which grouped the organisms into one of four

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:59.520
types that seemed to largely correspond to
how they move: sporozoa, flagellates, ciliates,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.690
and amoeba.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:06.860
According to this classification, amoebas
are the ones with pseudopodia, extensions

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:12.489
of the cytoplasm that help the amoeba eat
and move around.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:19.170
Amoeboid protists were largely grouped together
into a class or phylum called Sarcodina, a

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:24.719
name derived from the word “sarcode,”
which was in turn a French version of the

00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:29.340
Greek word for “fleshy.”

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.060
These classifications were built on the tools
of the time: namely, microscopes and what

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.290
scientists could observe through them.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:43.090
And so the pseudopodia that defined amoebas
were also used to differentiate them from

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.319
one another.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:50.290
Some of these pseudopodia are likely what
you would imagine, loose and spreading.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:58.831
But heliozoa are also amoebas, their bodies
extending outward in distinct, spiking, rays

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:03.909
that are actually pseudopodia held rigid by
inner microtubules.

00:03:03.909 --> 00:03:11.769
And the bodies of amoebas, despite the collective
blob we often envision, show the same range.

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:19.950
Some amoebas, called testate amoebas, actually
build shells around themselves, they’re

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.549
single-celled constructors.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:27.810
These shells vary across species in how they’re
made and what they look like.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:34.510
This young Arcella is colorless, but as it
ages, the storage of iron and manganese in

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.519
its shell will turn it brown.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:43.249
Unfortunately, while the shells of testate
amoeba can be stunning, they can also be difficult

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:51.300
to record because the weight of the glass
coverslip on the slide can break those shells.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:05.349
In terms of size, amoebas can range from microns
to centimeters, making them sometimes visible

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:07.780
to our naked eyes.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.629
The amoeba Pelomyxa is somewhere in between.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:16.450
But even though they usually measure in a
little under 1 millimeter, they’re still

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.660
considered a “giant amoeba”.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:26.370
To get a sense of just how big a giant is
in the microcosmos, here’s a Pelomyxa…and

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.020
here’s a tardigrade.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:38.140
Remember, tardigrades have thousands of cells,
and that Pelomyxa has just the one.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:45.600
Inside of that one giant cell is a body lacking
in many organelles we associate with eukaryotes:

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.060
there is no mitochondria powering the cell,
no Golgi body moving things around.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:58.800
It does, however, house endosymbiotic bacteria,
and multiple nuclei.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:06.100
While some Pelomyxa might have only a few
nuclei, others have been found with several

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.480
thousand.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:15.870
The simplicity of the Pelomyxa body was for
a long time the basis of the belief that it

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:21.250
is primitive, predating the advent of those
other eukaryotic parts.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:29.150
The truth, as we will see later, gets murkier
as we try to piece together two billion years

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:34.090
of eukaryotic history.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:40.850
Amoebas move in a way that is, very helpfully,
described as amoeboid locomotion, a combination

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:44.440
of extending pseudopodia and altered shape.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:50.500
And as this single-celled creature makes its
way around in that particularly amoeboid way,

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:57.090
you should know that this kind of movement
is going on inside of you right now.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:03.630
It’s driving your white blood cells around,
for example, as they monitor for any external

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.520
threats.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:11.650
White blood cells share another amoeboid feature:
phagocytosis.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:16.940
Amoebas don’t have mouths, so when it comes
time to eat, they use their pseudopodia to

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:24.570
trap their prey and engulf it, drawing the
food into its own compartment called a vacuole,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:28.120
where it will then be digested.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:36.390
And yes, some species will feed on nerve tissue,
including your brain, if the opportunity presents

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.390
itself.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:42.810
But the meal of choice for many amoebas is
not necessarily so terrifying.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:48.000
This Vampyrella, for instance, sounds menacing
because of its name.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.000
And sure, when it wants to feed, the Vampyrella
pokes a hole into its prey and begins to suck

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.250
out the contents.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:03.080
But its primary food is algae and sometimes
fungi, even the occasional microscopic worm.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:09.560
When they’re done eating, they form a cyst,
creating a wall around themselves as they

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:15.110
digest their meal and then divide.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:20.620
Given how important pseudopodia are to the
way amoebas live, it’s easy to understand

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.390
why they were used in our early attempts to
make sense of the microcosmos.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:31.960
But in more recent decades, as we’ve developed
the tools to investigate life based on DNA,

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.590
our categorization of amoebas has changed
as well.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:42.310
No longer lumped together in the Sarcodina,
most amoeboid protists are now divided between

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.720
two supergroups.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.270
One is Amoebozoa, which includes the Pelomyxa.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.750
The other group is Rhizaria, which includes
Heliozoa.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:58.250
But there are some lineages of amoebas that
weave their way out of these groups.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:05.640
As these changing evolutionary trees shift
our understanding of amoebas, they raise questions

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.580
about just what we call primitive.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:15.110
Pelomyxa, for example, was considered to be
among the earliest eukaryotes because they

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:17.190
lacked mitochondria.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:22.930
More recent evidence, however, indicates that
they’re actually the descendants of eukaryotes

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:24.730
that had mitochondria.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:29.730
It’s just that for some reason, Pelomyxa
seems to have lost theirs.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:35.630
And if someone would like to do some research
to try and determine why Pelomyxa is better

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:42.740
off without mitochondria, please do it, because
we are very curious.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:49.550
Even our understanding of how amoebas reproduce
has had to be corrected, our vision of a simple

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:57.699
organism simply dividing is now complicated
by interwoven lineages that mix sexual and

00:08:57.699 --> 00:08:59.459
asexual reproduction.

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:06.600
At the risk of getting too circular with our
references, our understanding of amoebas is…it’s

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:14.730
well…a lot like amoebas themselves: shifting,
full of splits and divisions, and yet somehow

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.920
encompassing.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:25.149
Amoebas are scattered now: left, right, up,
and down the evolutionary branches, sometimes

00:09:25.149 --> 00:09:31.759
as single cells, sometimes as parts of other
organisms, even us.

00:09:31.759 --> 00:09:38.360
It’s fitting then that we see amoebas as
useful touchstones or metaphors in books or

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:45.480
music or wherever else they show up; our bodies,
after all, are built out of the lessons that

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:52.089
nature learned with them.

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:57.399
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:09:57.399 --> 00:09:58.399
us.

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:04.689
And thank you especially for all of our patrons
on Patreon who let us do this wonderful thing.

00:10:04.689 --> 00:10:07.700
We hope that you enjoy it as much as we do.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.040
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James Weiss.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.360
Check our Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.640
